  (:adr/title "31 Hermes profiles become itonami CLI bots -- four pointers that pointed nowhere, and a lock that could never close")
  (:adr/source-format "edn")
  (:adr/body "# ADR-2609012300: Hermes bots become itonami CLI bots

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-09-01
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (owner instruction: 「hermes agent cli から itonami cli に
import して itonami cli bots を起動. itonami cloud, cli を最新の west pull, remote pull
して 機能を確認」)

## Result

All **31 Hermes profiles are provisioned as itonami bots** (`status provisioned`,
migration `hermes-25266ada-6bfd-4d8d-8c8e-97326f08b092`, 26,130 portable files /
2.11 GB, bundle at `data/bot-imports/`). `itonami bots list` reports 31 bots, all
enabled, all idle. A live turn was measured: `bots task --id <codinator> --text
自己紹介を一文で。` answered in 20 s, in persona, on top of 40 seeded messages of
its own redacted Hermes history.

**The schedules did not cross, deliberately.** `destination.activation` is
`interactive-ready-schedules-and-grants-not-activated`; `resident-job` is nil on
all 31; the compatibility matrix reports `automatic-source-schedule-activation`
and `source-grants` at 0.0. The cron plane is *in* the bundle
(`cron-jobs-schedules-and-health` via profile-export) as evidence. The 46 Hermes
cron jobs therefore still run in Hermes. Activating them in itonami is a separate
decision that needs the 96 `rebind-required` items (auth.json, provider/account
bindings, cloud-itonami grants) answered first -- source tool access is evidence
in the destination, not authority.

## Four pointers that pointed nowhere

ADR-2609012000 (yesterday) recorded that three pointers to \"the app\" were
unified via `~/.cloud-itonami/current`. **That directory does not exist on this
machine** -- the whole `~/.cloud-itonami` tree is absent. Measured today:

| pointer | was | now |
|---|---|---|
| PATH `itonami` | the **Hermes profile wrapper** (`hermes -p itonami`), written 2026-08-30 21:32 with the batch of hermes aliases | the app CLI (`bin/itonami`); the Hermes wrapper is `hermes-itonami` |
| `.mcp.json` | `cd $HOME/.cloud-itonami/current` -- nonexistent, hence CONNECTION_CLOSED | the west checkout; stdio `initialize` answers |
| launchd `cloud.itonami.app.local` | `clojure -M -m cloud.itonami.app.server` with **no provider credentials** | `nbb ~/.gftd/bin/itonami-app-resident.cljs`, which exports them |
| the checkout it all runs from | detached 105 commits behind the pin | at the pin |

The third was load-bearing and silent. `cloud.itonami.app.policy/credentialed?`
reads the env var a provider *declares*, not the declaration, so a resident
started without `MURAKUMO_API_KEY` reports `murakumo` as `credential-missing`
while it sits there `enabled? true, reviewed? true`. Before: the only admissible
provider was `ollama`. After: `murakumo` and `openrouter` both `allowed=true`.
**Bots provisioned an hour earlier would have been created against a server that
could not reach the fleet.** The secrets are not in the plist (0644 is not a
secret store); the wrapper reads a 0600 file and one Keychain item by exact
service name.

## The lock that could never close

`bots import hermes --stage true` failed twice, in two different ways.

**First**: `Hermes export が失敗しました`. Hermes's own `profile export` uses
`shutil.copytree`, which cannot copy a unix socket (`[Errno 102]`). Three
profiles with running gateways held `gateway.sock` and
`state/gateway.loop-tick.<pid>.sock`: codinator, hyakka-corpus, tabi-mikake. A
filesystem scan predicted exactly those three and the observed failures matched.
The `default` profile is immune because it exports a portable-roots whitelist
rather than the whole directory.

**Second**: `export 中に Hermes profile が変わりました。stage を破棄しました。`
`profile-inventory` hashes path+bytes+**mtime** of non-volatile files and
`stage!` re-inventories after the export. Two inventories taken 3 minutes apart
**with Hermes paused** differed in exactly one file across all 31 profiles:

    default  cron/jobs.json   size 192224 unchanged, mtime 1788270676149 -> 1788270796458

The cron ticker rewrites `jobs.json` about every two minutes with identical
bytes, and `hermes pause` does not stop the ticker -- it halts dispatch. The
export takes ~25 minutes. So on a live Hermes home the lock could never close.
`volatile-relative?` already excludes `ticker_heartbeat`, `ticker_last_success`,
`.tick.lock` and every `*.db`; `jobs.json` is the one live ticker file it does
not.

**What worked**: an APFS clone of `~/.hermes` (`cp -Rc`, clonefile, so disk
barely moved; `cp` skips sockets) and `bots import hermes --home <snapshot>`. A
frozen home has no ticker. Hermes was resumed and its three gateways came back
before the import ran, so production was not halted for the 25 minutes.

**Durable fix, not taken here**: either add `jobs.json` to the volatile leaf set,
or compare a content hash rather than mtime for it. Pausing is not the answer --
this ADR is the evidence that pausing does not make the source still.

## Sync

superproject ff'd to `origin/main` (15 behind, then 6 more). Three pins were
behind their upstream default tip and were advanced by server-side single-entry
commit: `cloud-itonami` 103862e->1e406e9 (+7), `cloud-murakumo-api`
4f27a48->500165b (+30), `cloud-itonami-app` 8fbbd5c->1dbd6e5 (+2).
`verify-west-pins` -> `変更された pin はありません。OK`.

itonami.cloud is healthy: `/`, `/.well-known/did.json`, `/llms.txt`,
`/api/onboarding/challenge` and `auth.itonami.cloud` all 200.
`POST api.murakumo.cloud/v1/messages` with the resident's credential returns 200
from `qwen3.8-27b-throughput-b70` -- ADR-2609012000's paren fix holds. Without a
credential it is 401, not 500; **a 401 alone would not have proven that**, which
is why the authenticated call was made.

## Other sessions' work, preserved not discarded

- `cloud-itonami-app`: 13 files / 532 insertions wiring `human-work-tools` into
  the bot tool surface. **Unlanded** -- `origin/main` has `human_work_tools.clj`
  but `bots.clj` does not reference it. Archived to
  `.git/stash-archive-20260901/` and stashed. The local `main` branch (1 ahead /
  362 behind, `Merge branch 'pr123'` from 2026-08-23) was kept as
  `main-local-20260823-pr123`.
- `cloud-murakumo-api`: OpenRouter attribution -> itonami.cloud, 3 files.
  **Superseded** -- main carries it with a refined title. Archived and stashed.

⚠ The first containment check on the second one was wrong and looked right: it
compared against `origin/main` in a west checkout whose remote is named for the
org, so `git show origin/main:<path>` resolved nothing and every file was
reported as differing. Re-run against `network-awai/main` it was superseded.
A containment check that cannot read its baseline reports the same shape as one
that read it and found a difference.

## Open

1. `~/.cloud-itonami` is gone entirely -- releases, `current`, and the `data`
   store ADR-2609012000's SLO figures (41.7% completion, 44 blocked) were
   measured against. The store now in use (`f370869d9d32`, in the checkout's
   `data/`) had **zero** bots and zero turns before today, so those numbers
   cannot be compared forward. Whether the old store should be recovered is an
   owner decision; the disk-reclamation work landed the same day
   (`576b4a3`, `e218187`) is the obvious suspect and was not investigated.
2. The SLO still reports `sample-size 0` / `completion-rate 0` after the live
   turn -- interactive turns do not appear to register in that window. Not
   diagnosed.
3. PATH `itonami` now hardcodes the west checkout path; it breaks if the
   workspace moves.
4. The 96 `rebind-required` items are unanswered, so no imported bot has
   provider credentials or grants of its own; they run on the destination's.
")))
